 ('expression-editing','Revise meaning, structure and language in separate passes','advanced',
  'Editing is easier when each pass has a purpose. First check meaning and missing conditions. Next check sentence order and paragraph focus. Then check agreement, tense, punctuation and word choice. Finally compare the revision with the original. A change is useful when it makes the intended meaning clearer; fewer words alone do not establish improvement.',
  'Draft: Because the learner had little time. She did a decision to skip all review. Revision: Because she had little time, the learner decided to shorten the review. The fragment and phrase are repaired, but skipping all review became shortening it: that meaning change needs the writer’s explicit approval.',
  'What must the editor notice about the worked revision?',
  ['It repairs language but changes skipping all review into shortening the review.','It preserves every detail exactly because it is more fluent.','A fluent sentence is always factually more accurate.','Only the number of words matters when comparing revisions.'],
  ['Both the repair and the changed action must be identified; fluency does not authorise a meaning change.','Fluency does not preserve the distinction between skipping and shortening.','Grammatical fluency cannot establish the factual accuracy of a statement.','Word count cannot show whether an action or qualification has been changed.'],
  'Write an original paragraph of at least 50 words about organising your next study session. In your review, name one meaning check, one structure check and one language check. Save a revision independently, then compare which changes improve clarity and which alter your intention.',
  ['Have I checked that the revision still says what I intended?','Can I explain a specific improvement without treating a shorter version as automatically better?']),
